.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-vm-create - Create a VM at an explicitly requested VMID, refusing a collision


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge vm create <target-id> <vmid> [field=value ...] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a VM at the VMID you name, from raw PVE create parameters.

.PP
The VMID is explicit and required — this command never picks one for you.
If that VMID is already taken, the command FAILS and names the id; it
never quietly substitutes a different one. A caller that asked to create
one VM must never discover it created one at an id it never saw.

.PP
pveforge does NOT inspect whether the existing VM's configuration
resembles what you asked to create. "Something already answers to this
VMID" is the entire collision test.

.PP
Create parameters are raw PVE fields (cores, memory, net0, ipconfig0,
scsi0, agent, tags, ...), given exactly like `vm set`: as
field=value arguments, or via --json / --json-file. Tags are not special
— pass tags=... as a create parameter and PVE applies them atomically as
part of the create itself, so the VM is never briefly untagged.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for create

.PP
\fB--json\fP=""
	JSON object of create parameters (values must be JSON strings)

.PP
\fB--json-file\fP=""
	path to a JSON file of create parameters (values must be JSON strings)

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-vm(1)\fP
